  - content: {fileID: 4900000, guid: 5c9cddbe49d9ee79f9044ef35c1810cb, type: 3}
    isChapterStart: 0
    chapter: 4
    generatedText: 
  pageBreakMarker: '[PAGE]'
  bookPages:
  - content: {fileID: 0}
    isChapterStart: 1
    chapter: 0
    generatedText: '<align=center><b>MINISTRY OF POSTAL COMMUNICATIONS</b>

      <b>CENTRAL
      TELEGRAPH DIRECTORATE</b>


      <b>OFFICIAL OPERATOR''S HANDBOOK</b>

      Edition
      1967 | Form M.P.C.-17


      <b>PROPERTY OF THE STATE</b>

      Unauthorized
      reproduction, alteration, or destruction of this handbook is prohibited.</align>'
  - content: {fileID: 0}
    isChapterStart: 0
    chapter: 0
    generatedText: '<size=120%><b>CHAPTER I - INTRODUCTION</b></size>

      <b>THE
      PRIVILEGE OF SERVICE</b>

      <b>CONGRATULATIONS, COMRADE!</b>


      You
      have been selected to join the elite ranks of the Ministry''s Postal Communications
      Corps.


      Of the thousands who apply, only the most disciplined, intelligent,
      and reliable citizens are entrusted with the sacred duty of directing the nation''s
      correspondence.'
  - content: {fileID: 0}
    isChapterStart: 0
    chapter: 0
    generatedText: 'Every telegram you process contributes to the efficiency, security,
      and prosperity of our great Union.


      <b>A MESSAGE DELAYED IS A NATION
      BETRAYED.</b>'
  - content: {fileID: 0}
    isChapterStart: 0
    chapter: 0
    generatedText: '<size=105%><b><color=#73654a>1.1 YOUR DUTIES</color></b></size>

      <b>I.
      TELEGRAPH RECEIVER</b>

      Receives encoded messages in International Morse
      Code. Each transmission begins with a three-letter identification code.

      <b>II.
      PUNCH-CARD STACK</b>

      Provides blank cards for operating Ministry machinery.
      Punch the appropriate holes according to this handbook.'
  - content: {fileID: 0}
    isChapterStart: 0
    chapter: 0
    generatedText: '<b>III. DECIPHERING APPARATUS</b>

      Accepts a prepared punch
      card and converts encrypted telegrams into readable instructions.

      <b>IV.
      DISPATCH TERMINAL</b>

      Accepts a second punch card specifying the destination,
      classification, handling procedure, and reference designation.

      <b>V.
      ROTARY TELEPHONE</b>

      Connects you to the appropriate department to confirm
      the completed dispatch.'
  - content: {fileID: 0}
    isChapterStart: 0
    chapter: 0
    generatedText: '<size=105%><b><color=#73654a>1.2 STANDARD OPERATING PROCEDURE</color></b></size>

      1.
      Receive the incoming Morse transmission.

      2. Identify the first three
      letters using the Morse reference chart.

      3. Prepare a deciphering punch
      card using the identification code and current date.

      4. Insert the card
      into the deciphering apparatus.

      5. Read the decrypted telegram.

      6.
      Prepare a dispatch punch card according to the telegram''s instructions.'
  - content: {fileID: 0}
    isChapterStart: 0
    chapter: 0
    generatedText: '7. Insert the card into the dispatch terminal.

      8. Dial
      the appropriate departmental telephone number.

      9. Confirm the completed
      transmission.


      <b>DO NOT IMPROVISE. DO NOT ASSUME. FOLLOW PROCEDURE.</b>'
  - content: {fileID: 0}
    isChapterStart: 0
    chapter: 0
    generatedText: '<size=110%><b><color=#73654a>1.3 PERFORMANCE AND REMUNERATION</color></b></size>

      A
      transmission is evaluated only upon final confirmation. No additional compensation
      is awarded for speed.


      Each working day is strictly limited. Any unfinished
      transmissions remaining at the end of your shift will be transferred to the
      night shift for processing, without penalty to your performance record.'
  - content: {fileID: 0}
    isChapterStart: 1
    chapter: 1
    generatedText: '<size=120%><b>CHAPTER II - MORSE CODE</b></size>

      All incoming
      communications arrive in International Morse Code.


      <size=120%>

      DOT:
      . (short signal)

      DASH: - (long signal)

      </size>


      Each letter
      is separated by a space. Words are separated by /. Line breaks indicate separate
      lines of the telegram.'
  - content: {fileID: 0}
    isChapterStart: 0
    chapter: 1
    generatedText: '<size=110%><b><color=#73654a>2.1 INTERNATIONAL MORSE ALPHABET</color></b></size>

      <size=120%>A
      .-<pos=35%>J .---<pos=70%>S ...

      B -...<pos=35%>K -.-<pos=70%>T -

      C
      -.-.<pos=35%>L .-..<pos=70%>U ..-

      D -..<pos=35%>M --<pos=70%>V ...-

      E
      .<pos=35%>N -.<pos=70%>W .--

      F ..-.<pos=35%>O ---<pos=70%>X -..-

      G
      --.<pos=35%>P .--.<pos=70%>Y -.--

      H ....<pos=35%>Q --.-<pos=70%>Z --..

      I
      ..<pos=35%>R .-.

      </size>'
  - content: {fileID: 0}
    isChapterStart: 0
    chapter: 1
    generatedText: '<size=110%><b><color=#73654a>2.2 NUMERICAL SIGNALS</color></b></size>

      <size=120%>0
      -----<pos=50%>5 .....

      1 .----<pos=50%>6 -....

      2 ..---<pos=50%>7
      --...

      3 ...--<pos=50%>8 ---..

      4 ....-<pos=50%>9 ----.

      </size>

      <size=110%><b><color=#73654a>2.3
      ADDITIONAL SYMBOLS</color></b></size>

      <size=120%>Colon (:)  ---...

      Hyphen
      (-) -....-

      Word separator: /

      New line: actual line break

      </size>'
  - content: {fileID: 0}
    isChapterStart: 0
    chapter: 1
    generatedText: '<size=110%><b><color=#73654a>2.4 IDENTIFYING THE TRANSMISSION</color></b></size>

      Every
      incoming telegram begins with a three-letter identification code.


      These
      first three letters are transmitted without encryption.


      The remaining
      message is encrypted and must be processed by the deciphering apparatus.'
  - content: {fileID: 0}
    isChapterStart: 0
    chapter: 1
    generatedText: '<b>EXAMPLE</b>

      -.- --- --

      Decode each letter individually:

      <b>-.-
      = K</b>

      <b>--- = O</b>

      <b>-- = M</b>

      <b>IDENTIFICATION
      CODE: KOM</b>


      Record these letters carefully. They determine the required
      deciphering punch card.'
  - content: {fileID: 0}
    isChapterStart: 1
    chapter: 2
    generatedText: '<size=120%><b>CHAPTER III - DECIPHERING</b></size>

      To
      protect the nation''s correspondence, the Ministry employs mechanical encryption.


      Each
      transmission requires a deciphering punch card prepared from its identification
      code and the current day of the month.'
  - content: {fileID: 0}
    isChapterStart: 0
    chapter: 2
    generatedText: '<size=110%><b><color=#73654a>3.1 THE PUNCH CARD</color></b></size>

      Every
      standard Ministry punch card contains 4 vertical columns, 6 numbered rows,
      and 24 possible punching positions.


      For standard deciphering operations,
      punch exactly one hole in each column.

      Column 1: First identification
      letter

      Column 2: Second identification letter

      Column 3: Third
      identification letter

      Column 4: Current day of the month'
  - content: {fileID: 0}
    isChapterStart: 0
    chapter: 2
    generatedText: 'Additional holes may alter the deciphering result. Incorrectly
      punched cards should be discarded and replaced.


      <size=110%><b><color=#73654a>3.2
      IDENTIFICATION LETTER TABLE</color></b></size>

      <size=120%>Row 1: A G
      M S Y

      Row 2: B H N T Z

      Row 3: C I O U

      Row 4: D J P V

      Row
      5: E K Q W

      Row 6: F L R X

      </size>'
  - content: {fileID: 0}
    isChapterStart: 0
    chapter: 2
    generatedText: '<size=110%><b><color=#73654a>3.3 DATE TABLE</color></b></size>

      <size=120%>Row
      1: 1, 7, 13, 19, 25, 31

      Row 2: 2, 8, 14, 20, 26

      Row 3: 3, 9,
      15, 21, 27

      Row 4: 4, 10, 16, 22, 28

      Row 5: 5, 11, 17, 23, 29

      Row
      6: 6, 12, 18, 24, 30

      </size>


      Always use the current working
      date.'
  - content: {fileID: 0}
    isChapterStart: 1
    chapter: 3
    generatedText: '<size=120%><b>CHAPTER IV - DISPATCH</b></size>

      Once the
      telegram has been deciphered, its instructions must be transferred to a dispatch
      punch card. A standard telegram contains four fields:

      <b>DESTINATION</b>:
      Receiving department

      <b>TYPE</b>: Classification of correspondence

      <b>HANDLING</b>:
      Required processing procedure

      <b>REFERENCE</b>: Administrative reference
      designation'
  - content: {fileID: 0}
    isChapterStart: 0
    chapter: 3
    generatedText: '<size=110%><b><color=#73654a>4.1 DISPATCH CARD CONFIGURATION</color></b></size>

      Column
      1: Destination

      Column 2: Mail type

      Column 3: Handling

      Column
      4: Reference


      For standard dispatch operations, punch exactly one row
      per column.'
  - content: {fileID: 0}
    isChapterStart: 0
    chapter: 3
    generatedText: '<size=110%><b><color=#73654a>4.2 DESTINATION TABLE</color></b></size>

      1
      CIVILIAN

      2 GOVERNMENT

      3 MILITARY

      4 INDUSTRY

      5 INTELLIGENCE

      6
      INTERNATIONAL

      <size=110%><b><color=#73654a>4.3 MAIL TYPE TABLE</color></b></size>

      1
      PERSONAL

      2 OFFICIAL

      3 CLASSIFIED

      4 COMMERCIAL

      5
      DIPLOMATIC

      6 RESTRICTED'
  - content: {fileID: 0}
    isChapterStart: 0
    chapter: 3
    generatedText: '<size=110%><b><color=#73654a>4.4 HANDLING TABLE</color></b></size>

      1
      DELIVER

      2 HOLD

      3 INSPECT

      4 RETURN

      5 FORWARD

      6
      DESTROY

      <size=110%><b><color=#73654a>4.5 REFERENCE TABLE</color></b></size>

      1
      A

      2 B

      3 C

      4 D

      5 E

      6 F'
  - content: {fileID: 0}
    isChapterStart: 1
    chapter: 4
    generatedText: '<size=120%><b>CHAPTER V | TELEPHONE</b></size>

      Every completed
      dispatch must be confirmed by telephone.


      The rotary telephone connects
      the operator to the Ministry''s authorized receiving departments.


      The
      correct telephone number is determined exclusively by the telegram''s DESTINATION
      field.'
  - content: {fileID: 0}
    isChapterStart: 0
    chapter: 4
    generatedText: '<size=110%><b><color=#73654a>5.1 OFFICIAL TELEPHONE DIRECTORY</color></b></size>

      <size=120%>Civilian
      Postal Office: 101

      Government Bureau: 204

      Military Command: 315

      Industrial
      Administration: 426

      Intelligence Directorate: 537

      International
      Dispatch: 648

      </size>'
  - content: {fileID: 0}
    isChapterStart: 0
    chapter: 4
    generatedText: '<size=110%><b><color=#73654a>5.2 DIALING PROCEDURE</color></b></size>

      1.
      Verify the destination indicated by the deciphered telegram.

      2. Locate
      the corresponding department in the official directory.

      3. Dial all
      three digits using the rotary telephone.

      4. Confirm the completed dispatch.


      The
      Ministry will evaluate the submitted dispatch instructions and telephone destination.'
  - content: {fileID: 0}
    isChapterStart: 0
    chapter: 4
    generatedText: '<size=120%><b>CREDITS</b></size>

      <b>ACKNOWLEDGMENTS AND
      OFFICIAL NOTICE</b>


      <b>MINISTRY OF POSTAL COMMUNICATIONS</b>

      Official
      Operator''s Handbook | 1967 Edition


      COMMIE-nication

      A game about
      messages, machinery, and obedience.


      Developed by: DotDamageGames

      Programming:
      DotDamageGames

      Art and Interface: DotDamageGames

      Font: Google
      Font

      Audio: [Credits]'
  - content: {fileID: 0}
    isChapterStart: 0
    chapter: 4
    generatedText: 'This handbook and its fictional institutions are part of the
      game world.


      <b>REMEMBER, COMRADE:</b>


      The nation speaks through
      its wires.

      The operator ensures that it is heard.


      <b>END OF
      HANDBOOK</b>


      <b>RETURN TO YOUR POST.</b>'
  currentPage: 0
  displayedLeft:
    content: {fileID: 0}
    isChapterStart: 0
    chapter: 0
    generatedText: 
  displayedRight:
    content: {fileID: 0}
    isChapterStart: 0
    chapter: 0
    generatedText: 
